<#
.SYNOPSIS
    Build a static site and publish it to a remote nginx container by scp (push model).

.DESCRIPTION
    Copy to `publish.ps1` in your app repo and adapt the CONFIG block below. This is the PUSH
    publish model: build locally, then scp the output to the box's bind-mount directory. Use it
    when the box cannot (or should not) hold a clone of the repo -- a private repo you can't or
    won't give the box a read-only deploy key for, or a box with no repo access. If the box CAN
    clone the repo directly, prefer the pull model instead (publish/deploy-pull.sh + the systemd
    timer units).

    Template, incl. the "Push or pull?" section behind that choice -- relative paths won't resolve
    from your repo:
      https://github.com/Rackbops/rackbops-web-deploy-template/blob/main/servers/nginx-static/README.md#push-or-pull
    What each kind of change needs on the box:
      https://github.com/Rackbops/rackbops-web-deploy-template/blob/main/servers/nginx-static/README.md#updating

    Content-only, and content needs no restart -- this script never ships nginx.conf. What each
    kind of change does need is in the "Updating" section linked above, not restated here.
    If your build hashes asset filenames (Vite etc.), a fresh index.html always references
    the assets that shipped alongside it, so there's no stale-asset window.

    Publishes via a remote STAGING directory, not a direct scp into the live web-root dir:

      1. scp the build into a throwaway "<dir>.new" next to the live dir. The network transfer --
         the slow, failure-prone step -- happens here, and the live dir is untouched while it runs.
      2. Only once that fully succeeds, swap it in with a single ssh call doing a local (same-host,
         no-network) delete-then-copy -- so the window where the live dir is momentarily empty is
         milliseconds, not however long an scp over the network takes.

    Why this shape (all proven the hard way on the reference implementation, Rackbops/Tooling's
    tools-site):
      * A network blip or interrupted scp leaves the live site exactly as it was -- it never
        empties the live dir with nothing to replace it.
      * The live dir's own directory ENTRY is kept, never removed-and-recreated -- the container's
        bind mount is a live reference to that directory; replacing the directory itself (vs. its
        contents) risks orphaning the mount.
      * scp's source is "<dir>/." (trailing dot), not "<dir>/": `scp -r <dir> host:existing-dir/`
        NESTS (creates a "<dir>" folder inside the target) whenever the target already exists,
        which the staging dir always does. The trailing-dot form copies the CONTENTS instead.
      * $LocalDirName/$RemoteDirName/$RemoteDir are validated against an allowlist before anything
        runs -- it rejects a value that could inject a remote command (a "'" breaking out of the
        single-quoting in the command strings below) or traverse out of its subtree (a stray '..',
        or a '/' where a single dir name is expected). Rejected loudly, before any ssh/scp call.
        It does NOT judge where a validly-spelled target POINTS, though: $RemoteDir='/opt/stacks'
        with $RemoteDirName='<app>' is legal and composes onto the stack dir itself. The swap guards
        that (next bullet), not this allowlist.
      * The swap verifies the staged build actually holds a site (`index.html` present) AND that the
        live dir is not a Dockge stack dir (no compose.yaml in it -- every stack dir has one) before
        touching it -- an scp that exits 0 with an unexpected layout (nesting, a modified call site),
        or a $RemoteDir/$RemoteDirName pair that composed onto the stack dir, refuses instead of
        wiping compose.yaml/nginx.conf/deploy/ for nothing. The guard's edges, all by design: it does
        NOT protect a target aimed at a not-yet-populated stack dir (no compose.yaml there yet --
        normal setup places it before the first publish), nor a stack dir whose compose file is named
        compose.yml/docker-compose.yaml rather than compose.yaml, nor a compose onto the stacks-ROOT
        (/opt/stacks) itself, which holds no compose.yaml of its own -- the guard checks the target
        dir, not its parent. And it OVER-refuses one legit shape: a real web root that itself serves a
        top-level compose.yaml reads as a stack dir and is refused (rename that asset).
      * Explicit $LASTEXITCODE checks after each native call: PowerShell only auto-throws on a
        native nonzero exit under 7.4+ with $PSNativeCommandUseErrorActionPreference on. The
        checks make failure loud on any pwsh.
      * Uses only ordinary coreutils on the box (find, cp -a, rm -rf) -- no rsync (not always
        installed).

    Connection reuse (optional): a publish opens several separate ssh/scp connections to $Server --
    the staging setup, the transfer, the swap, and the cleanup -- each paying its own TCP + SSH
    handshake and auth round-trip. On a high-latency link, a jump host, or a server with 2FA/
    certificate challenges, that cost adds up for a script meant to be run often. Add a
    ControlMaster stanza for this box's Host to your ~/.ssh/config and scp plus the later ssh calls
    will reuse the first connection instead of renegotiating:

      Host <host>
        ControlMaster auto
        ControlPersist 10m
        ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm-%r@%h:%p
        ServerAliveInterval 15
        ServerAliveCountMax 3

    A long username or a deep-subdomain host can push that ControlPath past the OS's ~100-byte
    Unix-socket path limit, silently defeating the whole stanza -- swap in `%C` (a hash of user,
    host, and port) instead of `%r@%h:%p` if `<host>`/your username are long. The keepalive lines
    matter here too: without them a silently-dead connection (NAT/VPN drop, box reboot) can leave a
    stale master that a later publish hangs against instead of cleanly reconnecting.

    Windows caveat: which ssh.exe this script's `& ssh`/`& scp` calls actually invoke depends on
    PATH order -- run `Get-Command ssh` to check. If it resolves to the Windows-optional-feature
    client (`C:\Windows\System32\OpenSSH\ssh.exe`, "Win32-OpenSSH"), do NOT add this stanza: that
    client doesn't gracefully ignore ControlPath, it errors out ("getsockname failed: Not a
    socket") and aborts the connection -- turning every ssh/scp call in this script into a hard
    failure, not a missed optimization. If `Get-Command ssh` instead resolves to Git for Windows'
    own bundled OpenSSH build (common when Git for Windows is on PATH ahead of System32), or the
    calls run under WSL/Git Bash/a Linux/macOS client, ControlMaster generally works as intended.
    When in doubt, test one `ssh -o ControlMaster=auto -o ControlPath=~/.ssh/cm-test <host>` by
    hand before wiring the stanza into your `~/.ssh/config`.

.PARAMETER Server
    SSH target for the box (user@host).

.PARAMETER RemoteDir
    The app's stack directory on the box (holds compose.yaml + nginx.conf, shipped once at setup,
    plus the web-root dir this script reships every run).

.EXAMPLE
    .\publish.ps1
#>
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    [string] $Server = '<user>@<host>',
    [string] $RemoteDir = '/opt/stacks/<app>'
)

Set-StrictMode -Version Latest
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

# === CONFIG: adapt these two to your app ===
$LocalDirName = 'dist'    # the build output dir under this repo (e.g. dist, build; or 'site' if there's no build)
$RemoteDirName = 'dist'   # the web-root dir name under $RemoteDir on the box -- a single subdirectory
                          # name, NOT the stack dir itself or the repo root. If $RemoteDir/$RemoteDirName
                          # compose onto the stack dir, the swap refuses it (it won't wipe a dir that
                          # holds a compose.yaml -- every stack dir has one); the repo-root mount is pull-only.
# ===========================================

# Allowlist, not a blocklist: $RemoteDirName/$LocalDirName/$RemoteDir all end up single-quoted
# inside the remote command strings below (e.g. '$remoteLive'), so a stray "'" -- not just '/' or
# '\' -- would break out of that quoting and inject an arbitrary remote command. Restricting to
# a closed character set closes that off instead of trying to blocklist every shell metacharacter.
function Assert-SafeName {
    param([string] $Name, [string] $Value, [switch] $AllowSlash)
    if ($AllowSlash) {
        $ok = $Value -match '^/?[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9._/-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?$'
        $allowed = "letters, digits, '.', '_', '-', '/'"
        # The character class alone doesn't forbid a '..' PATH SEGMENT (e.g. '/opt/../etc') --
        # check each '/'-separated segment explicitly so a traversal can't hide inside an
        # otherwise-legal string.
        if ($ok -and ($Value -split '/' | Where-Object { $_ -eq '..' })) {
            $ok = $false
        }
    } else {
        $ok = $Value -match '^[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9._-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?$'
        $allowed = "letters, digits, '.', '_', '-' (no '/' or '\')"
    }
    if (-not $ok) {
        throw "`$$Name is '$Value' -- must contain only $allowed, start/end with a letter or digit, and contain no '..' path segment (no quotes, spaces, or other characters a remote shell could interpret)"
    }
}
Assert-SafeName -Name 'LocalDirName' -Value $LocalDirName
Assert-SafeName -Name 'RemoteDirName' -Value $RemoteDirName
Assert-SafeName -Name 'RemoteDir' -Value $RemoteDir -AllowSlash

$localPath = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot $LocalDirName

# === BUILD: adapt (or delete) this block ===
# The reference implementation (tools-site) runs `npm run build --prefix $PSScriptRoot`, which
# runs the build with its cwd set to this repo regardless of where you invoke the script from.
# Replace the command to match your build; DELETE the whole block if the site is prebuilt or has
# no build step (e.g. a hand-authored one-pager). Keep the $LASTEXITCODE check so a failed build
# never publishes a stale/empty output.
Write-Output "Building (npm run build)..."
& npm run build --prefix $PSScriptRoot
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {
    throw "build failed (exit $LASTEXITCODE)"
}
# ===========================================

$localIndex = Join-Path $localPath 'index.html'
if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $localIndex -PathType Leaf)) {
    throw "No $LocalDirName/index.html at $localIndex -- refusing to publish an empty/incomplete build"
}

$remoteLive    = "$RemoteDir/$RemoteDirName"
$remoteStaging = "$RemoteDir/$RemoteDirName.new"

Write-Output "Staging at $Server`:$remoteStaging..."
& ssh $Server "rm -rf '$remoteStaging' && mkdir -p '$remoteStaging'"
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {
    throw "ssh staging setup at '$remoteStaging' on $Server failed (exit $LASTEXITCODE)"
}

$stagingTarget = "${Server}:${remoteStaging}/"
Write-Output "Copying $localPath -> $stagingTarget"
& scp -r (Join-Path $localPath '.') $stagingTarget
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {
    throw "scp to $stagingTarget failed (exit $LASTEXITCODE) -- the live site at '$remoteLive' was not touched"
}

Write-Output "Swapping the staged build into $Server`:$remoteLive (local copy on the box, no network)..."
& ssh $Server "test -f '$remoteStaging/index.html' && mkdir -p '$remoteLive' && test ! -e '$remoteLive/compose.yaml' && find '$remoteLive' -mindepth 1 -delete && cp -a '$remoteStaging'/. '$remoteLive'/"
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {
    throw "swap into '$remoteLive' on $Server failed (exit $LASTEXITCODE) -- one of: the staged build failed verification (no index.html); OR '$remoteLive' already holds a compose.yaml, so it reads as a Dockge stack dir and the swap refused to wipe it -- either `$RemoteDir/`$RemoteDirName are misset and compose onto the stack dir (they compose to '$remoteLive'; publish into a web-root SUBDIR instead), or this really is your web root and it serves a top-level compose.yaml (rename that asset); OR the swap failed partway (live site may be inconsistent). In the first two cases the live site was NOT touched. The staged build is left at '$remoteStaging' on the box -- inspect it, then re-run this script"
}

# Cleanup is deliberately a separate, non-fatal call: by this point the live dir is already
# correct, so a failure here (e.g. a transient permission issue) must not be reported as "the
# swap may be inconsistent" -- it's just a leftover staging dir, harmless and self-cleaned by the
# next publish's staging setup above.
& ssh $Server "rm -rf '$remoteStaging'"
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {
    Write-Warning "Published, but cleanup of '$remoteStaging' on $Server failed (exit $LASTEXITCODE) -- the live site is correct; the leftover staging dir is harmless and will be replaced by the next publish."
}

# === POST-ARRIVAL (per-server) ================================================================
# The CODE above is transport: build, stage, swap, all server-agnostic. What follows is not -- it
# states what serves the files. A second base server would swap this block. (The .DESCRIPTION at
# the top names nginx too; the seam this marker draws is in the executable part, not the docs.)
Write-Output "Published. Content-only changes are live immediately -- no restart needed."
Write-Output "(A restart is only required when nginx.conf changes, which this script never ships.)"
